# What a clause boundary owes when more than one condition is waiting, pinned
# on the tree-walker's own bytes.
#
# `corpus/lang/condition_queue_drain.rex` holds the same subject against the
# live oracle, and it reaches only the engine `rexx-run` defaults to. These
# stanzas are what says the tree-walker produces the same thing.
#
# **The rule has two halves and each stanza needs both.** A boundary takes
# everything the clause left pending, in the order it was queued; what a
# handler queues *while it runs* is owed to the next boundary instead. Draining
# without the deferral runs a requeued handler at the clause that delivered its
# predecessor; deferring without the drain answers the last condition only and
# loses the rest, which is what a single-slot `pending_trap` did.
#
# Every expected byte below was measured against the oracle before it was
# written here.

# Two conditions queued by one clause. Both handlers are owed to that clause's
# own boundary, in the order the conditions were raised, and both report the
# raising clause's line rather than their own.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
zn = ra() + rb()
say 'after' zn
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
rb:
raise user zy return 2
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
return
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h ran 3
out> g ran 3
out> after 3

# The pairing neither half of the rule produces alone. Pass one's body clause
# queues, its handler requeues, and that requeue waits for the `END`; the
# `END`'s handler requeues again, so pass two's body clause owes **two**
# handlers -- the one left over and the one it queued itself -- both at its own
# line. That clause is `zn = ra()`; the `do` above it and the `end` below it are
# the neighbouring lines, which is what makes `h ran 5` / `g ran 6` read as body
# clause then `END`. An engine that drains without deferring runs `g` at the
# body clause's line on the first pass; one that defers without draining never
# runs `k` there at all.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
call on user zw name k
do zi = 1 to 2
zn = ra()
end
say 'after' zn
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
raise user zw return 1
k:
say 'k ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h ran 5
out> g ran 6
out> k ran 5
out> h ran 5
out> g ran 6
out> after 1
out> k ran 7
